4. Core Data Inputs

The system has four primary inputs.

4.1 Demand

Demand is provided by Sales.

The production analyst does not generate or modify demand.

Demand is treated as an input to the production-planning process.

The planning horizon will initially contain multiple monthly periods, for example:

September
October
November
December
January
February
March

The exact planning horizon can be configured later.

Principle

Sales owns demand. Production Planning analyzes demand against available supply.

4.2 NetSuite Inventory

NetSuite provides the on-hand inventory dump.

Inventory must retain location-level information.

Example:

SKU	Location	On Hand
CAM001-EU	Europe	800
CAM001-EU	Canada	50
CAM001-CA	Canada	600

The planning engine must not blindly aggregate all inventory for a SKU.

Inventory eligibility must consider the SKU's intended market/location.

This prevents a major failure mode from the original process:

European demand being satisfied by inventory that belongs to Canada.

4.3 In-Transit

In-transit inventory is initially treated as a separate input.

It may be maintained manually by the logistics team.

Example:

SKU	Destination	Quantity	Expected Date
CAM001-EU	Europe	500	2026-09-15
CAM001-CA	Canada	500	2026-10-01

The automation will use this information when calculating available supply.

The initial prototype will not attempt to automate the creation of in-transit data.

4.4 SKU Master

The SKU Master provides the business meaning of each SKU.

It is a critical reference dataset.

Potential fields include:

Field	Purpose
SKU	Unique product identifier
SKU Description	Product description
Product Type	Camera / Accessory / Bundle / etc.
Package Type	Single / Twin / 3-Pack / 4-Pack / Kit / Costco Bundle
Market	EU / CA / US / etc.
Manufacturing Site	Manufacturing location
Contract Manufacturer	China / India
Status	Active / Discontinued
Effective Date	Optional future use
BOM Reference	Future use

The SKU Master allows the automation to understand the business meaning of a SKU instead of relying only on SKU numbers.

5. SKU and Location Logic

SKU identification and location eligibility are separate concepts.

The SKU Master defines the intended market and business characteristics of the SKU.

The inventory source defines where physical inventory currently exists.

The planning engine combines both.

SKU MASTER
    |
    |-- Market / intended destination
    |
    v
INVENTORY
    |
    |-- Physical location
    |
    v
ELIGIBLE INVENTORY

The system must validate that inventory being used to satisfy demand is appropriate for that SKU/market.

This is a core business rule and must not be implemented as an uncontrolled spreadsheet SUMIF.

6. Planning Engine

The planning engine determines the production requirement for each finished good.

The fundamental planning relationship is:

Net Requirement
=
Demand
-
Eligible On-Hand Inventory
-
Relevant In-Transit
+
Required Safety Stock

The exact safety-stock calculation used in the original Vosker process is not yet known and will be documented separately in BUSINESS_RULES.md.

The automation must not invent a new safety-stock methodology without an explicit business decision.

7. Production Quantity

Once the net requirement is determined, the system will calculate a production recommendation.

Production quantities must respect manufacturing constraints.

For the initial prototype:

China MOQ = 500
India MOQ = 500

Production quantities will therefore be rounded according to the applicable production multiple/MOQ.

Example:

Calculated requirement = 620
MOQ / production multiple = 500

Recommended production = 1,000

The exact rounding rule will be documented in BUSINESS_RULES.md.

8. Low-Demand / MOQ Exception

A production recommendation should not blindly trigger production when demand is very low.

Example:

4-month demand = 120
MOQ = 500

The system should be capable of identifying this as a planning exception.

Example planner message:

LOW DEMAND / MOQ REVIEW

Demand over planning horizon: 120
Production MOQ: 500
Recommended action: Planner review before production
Reason: Producing MOQ may create excess inventory.

The threshold for this exception will be configurable rather than hardcoded.

9. Discontinued SKU Logic

Discontinued SKUs must not receive a normal production recommendation.

Basic rule:

IF SKU Status = Discontinued
AND Demand > 0

THEN
    Production Quantity = 0
    Create Planner Exception

Example:

Exception:
DISCONTINUED SKU — DEMAND EXISTS

SKU: CAM002-EU
Demand: 350
Production Recommendation: 0
Planner Action: Review remaining demand / inventory strategy

The automation may visually highlight discontinued SKUs in the FG output.

However:

Colour is presentation. The actual planning decision must be based on the SKU status field.

10. Exceptions

The system will maintain a separate Exceptions output.

This prevents the main FG Planning Master from becoming overloaded with comments and manual notes.

Potential exception types include:

Discontinued SKU with demand
Missing SKU Master record
Market/location mismatch
Missing inventory
Missing in-transit information
Low demand vs MOQ
New SKU requiring validation
Unexpected production requirement
Other data-quality exceptions

Example:

Exception Type	SKU	Market	Demand	Production	Planner Action
Discontinued SKU	CAM002	EU	350	0	Review
MOQ Risk	CAM003	CA	120	500	Review excess
Missing SKU Master	CAM004	US	800	—	Validate master
Location Mismatch	CAM005	EU	500	—	Validate inventory

The exception layer is where human intervention should be concentrated.

11. FG Planning Master

The automation will generate an FG Planning Master based on the input datasets.

The FG structure should retain the business format used in the original planning process.

Example:

SKU	SKU Desc	Type	Sep	Oct	Nov	Dec	Jan	Feb	Mar
CAM001	Camera X	Single	...	...	...	...	...	...	...

For each SKU, the FG output will contain the relevant planning information required to understand the production recommendation.

The final column structure will be defined in DATA_DICTIONARY.md.

12. CM File Generation

The FG Planning Master is not the same as the file sent to the Contract Manufacturer.

The automation will create the CM files after the FG planning calculation.

FG PLANNING MASTER
        |
        v
CM ALLOCATION
        |
        +------------------+
        |                  |
        v                  v
   CHINA CM FILE      INDIA CM FILE

The CM file will contain only the products/quantities relevant to that manufacturing partner.

The allocation will be based on SKU Master manufacturing information.

The initial design should support:

China CM
India CM

without creating separate planning logic for each CM.

13. Product / Package Types

The system must support different finished-good configurations.

Examples include:

Single camera
Twin pack
3-pack
4-pack
Amazon kits
Costco bundles
Accessories

Different package types may represent different quantities of underlying units.

For example:

Twin Pack
= 2 units

3-Pack
= 3 units

Single
= 1 unit

The package/unit conversion logic will be documented separately.

BOM explosion and component planning are not part of the first implementation.

14. Future BOM Layer

BOM processing will be added after the finished-goods planning engine is working correctly.

Future architecture:

FG Production Recommendation
             |
             v
         BOM Explosion
             |
             v
       Component Demand
             |
             v
     Component Planning

This will allow the automation to eventually determine component requirements based on finished-goods production quantities.

BOM is intentionally excluded from the first version to keep the initial system focused and testable.

15. Daily Execution

The workflow is intended to run automatically on a daily schedule.

Conceptually:

