The system should produce a fresh planning output based on the latest available data.

Historical versions may be retained later for auditability and comparison.

16. Architecture Principles

The automation follows these principles:

16.1 Separate data from logic

Business rules should not be buried inside individual n8n nodes.

Where practical, rules such as:

MOQ
production multiples
SKU status
market mapping
manufacturing assignment

should be maintained as reference/configuration data.

16.2 Deterministic logic first

Production quantities are primarily a deterministic planning problem.

The system should use explicit supply-chain rules wherever possible.

AI should only be introduced where it provides genuine value, such as:

explaining an exception
summarizing unusual conditions
helping prioritize planner attention
interpreting unstructured communication

AI should not be used simply to make the project appear more "AI-powered."

16.3 Human judgment remains in the loop

The system recommends.

The planner remains responsible for decisions requiring business judgment.

Examples:

Low-demand MOQ decisions
Discontinued SKU demand
unusual inventory situations
market/location conflicts
exceptional production decisions
16.4 Modular workflow design

The n8n implementation should use modular sub-workflows where they improve maintainability.

Potential future sub-workflows include:

Retrieve Demand
Retrieve Inventory
Retrieve In-Transit
Validate SKU Master
Calculate Production Requirement
Generate CM File

The exact sub-workflow structure will be finalized during WORKFLOW_DESIGN.md.

16.5 Avoid recreating the Excel problem inside n8n

The objective is not:

"Take the giant Excel sheet and recreate it as 200 n8n nodes."

The objective is:

Separate data, rules, calculations, validation, exceptions, and outputs into simple reusable components.

